faq:
  - question: How much does a GEO agency cost in 2026?
    answer: >-
      Published prices in this ranking run from $997 a month to more than
      $20,000. Only one of those figures, Breadchaser.ai's $997, is
      attached to AI search work specifically. Four agencies publish no
      price at all. Any number you see on a directory is an estimate.
  - question: Can I do this with a tool instead of an agency?
    answer: >-
      For the first 90 days, usually yes. Tracking dashboards start around
      $95 a month and will tell you which prompts you appear in and which
      you do not. An agency earns its fee on the part software cannot do:
      getting mentioned on the third-party sources models cite, and doing
      it every month.
  - question: How long before AI search work shows results?
    answer: >-
      Between seven weeks and sixteen months, based on the dated case
      studies in this ranking. Optimist moved brand visibility from 1.1% to
      18% in seven weeks. Omniscient Digital moved LLM visibility from 31%
      to 55% in 60 days. Siege Media took roughly eight months.
  - question: Is GEO just SEO with a new name?
    answer: >-
      No, and there is a number behind that. Only about 17% of the sources
      cited in Google AI Overviews also rank in the organic top 10
      ([BrightEdge, February
      2026](https://www.brightedge.com/resources/weekly-ai-search-insights/ai-overviews-one-year-presence-size-citing)).
      Both need crawlable pages and topical depth. AI search adds
      prompt-level tracking, citation share by engine, and brand mentions
      on the sources models draw from.
  - question: Which engines should an agency track?
    answer: >-
      At minimum ChatGPT, Google AI Overviews, Perplexity and Gemini. An
      agency reporting one surface is reporting a fraction. Intero Digital
      publishes Google AI Overviews only. Embarque publishes Bing Copilot
      only.
  - question: Why are there only eleven agencies here?
    answer: >-
      Because thirteen of the twenty-four we checked could not show a
      result you could test. Five publish AI numbers with no measurement
      window and eight publish no AI result at all. A list of eleven
      verified agencies is worth more than a padded twenty.
---
## What AI search optimization actually costs

One agency in this ranking publishes a price for AI search work. Everyone else prices something broader and lets you assume it is included.

| Agency | Published figure | What that figure buys |
|---|---|---|
| Breadchaser.ai | $997/mo | AI search work. GEO and AEO named in the tier |
| Embarque | $1,500/mo | One SEO service pick. AI search is not on the menu |
| RevenueZen | $3,000/mo | Brand monitoring and a report. GEO starts at $10,000 |
| Optimist | $3,000/mo | Organic growth, with visibility tracking included |
| Siege Media | $8,000/mo | Content marketing. The GEO page has no price |
| Omniscient Digital | $10,000/mo | Content, SEO and AI search together |
| NoGood | $20,000/mo | Average across all growth marketing |
| Directive, Skale, Omnius, Intero Digital | Nothing published | Unknown |

The one clean price card we found belongs to an agency that did not make this ranking. [WebFX](https://www.webfx.com/seo/services/ai-search-optimization/) publishes a tier labelled GEO Services at $3,000 a month on a six-month commitment. Its AI result carries no timeframe, which is why it is not above.

Two questions get you past the brochure. Which tier includes the AI search work, and what happens if I buy the one below it.

## How to tell a real GEO agency from a renamed SEO page

Nearly every SEO agency added an AI search service page in the last eighteen months. Three checks separate the ones doing the work.

**Ask which engines they measure, and with what.** Google AI Overviews is not ChatGPT. Intero Digital publishes AI Overview data only. Embarque publishes Bing Copilot only. NoGood scores Perplexity, ChatGPT and Gemini separately. Siege Media is the only agency here that names its measurement tool, which was Peec AI.

**Ask what the starting number was.** "43% visibility" is not a result. "11% to 43% between October 2025 and June 2026" is. Four agencies in this ranking publish a before number. The rest publish only an after.

**Ask to speak to the client.** Skale, Optimist and Embarque anonymise every AI search client they write about. That is normal in enterprise work and it also means nothing in those case studies can be verified by you.

One more, quietly useful. Ask how many AI search case studies they have published in total. For most agencies here the answer is one.

## Should you do this in-house instead?

Often, yes, at least to start.

The first job in AI search is knowing where you stand, and that is a software problem, not an agency problem. Breadchaser.ai sells a tracking dashboard on its own for $95 a month. Several tools do the same thing. Ninety days of tracking tells you which prompts you already appear in, which competitors own the ones you do not, and whether your category produces AI traffic at all.

That is also the cheapest way to have a useful agency conversation. A buyer who walks in knowing their share of voice cannot be sold a discovery phase.

What an agency adds is the part software cannot do. Getting mentioned on the third-party sources models actually cite. Restructuring pages so an answer lifts cleanly. Doing both every month. If your team already produces content and owns technical SEO, you may only need help with the first of those.

Where in-house breaks down is publishing volume and outside coverage. AI engines cite sources they already trust. Building that from scratch is slow, and it is the reason the results in this ranking took between seven weeks and sixteen months.

## Is AI search traffic worth paying for yet?

It is small, and it converts well, and both of those things are true at once.

LLM referral traffic sits under 2% of total referral traffic, based on 13 months of data across one agency's client base to February 2026, and converts at roughly 18% ([Further, via Search Engine Land](https://searchengineland.com/what-13-months-of-data-reveals-about-llm-traffic-growth-and-conversions-470115)). Semrush puts the average AI search visitor at [4.4 times the value of an organic search visitor](https://www.semrush.com/blog/ai-seo-statistics/). Both figures come from single-company datasets, so treat them as direction rather than benchmark.

Meanwhile the surface keeps growing. 48% of tracked Google queries now trigger an AI Overview, up from 31% a year earlier ([BrightEdge, February 2026](https://www.brightedge.com/resources/weekly-ai-search-insights/ai-overviews-one-year-presence-size-citing)).

Three situations where waiting is the better call.

You have no organic presence yet. Every result in this ranking was built on an existing content base, not from nothing.

You cannot name the prompts you want to win. Track first, hire second.

You are below roughly $1M in annual recurring revenue and the cheapest real retainer here is $997 a month on a six-month minimum. That is $5,982 committed before anyone can tell you whether it is working.

## The agencies we cut, and why

Twenty-four agencies were checked. Thirteen were cut. Five publish AI numbers with no measurement window, and eight publish no AI result at all.

**Published a number, but no timeframe**

| Agency | What they publish | The problem |
|---|---|---|
| Victorious | 5,856 AI Overview citations | No window, no named client |
| WebFX | 586% traffic increase from generative AI for Thompson Tractor | No window |
| Single Grain | 400 AI citations, and 136 total AI visibility for two named clients | No window on either |
| Graphite | 94% share of voice growth from LLMs for Webflow | No window |
| NP Digital | 994% increase in AI referral traffic | No window, no named client |

**Published no AI result at all**

| Agency | The problem |
|---|---|
| iPullRank | Publishes investment ranges of $30K to $150K, $150K to $500K and $500K+ with no time unit anywhere on the page, so a buyer cannot tell whether that is monthly or yearly. No AI client result |
| First Page Sage | Claims its GEO service launched 9 May 2023 as the first ever. Supports it with one unattributed "+81% increase in SaaS client mentions in AI results", no client, no method, no window. Its own pages claim 15, 17 and 18 years of trading |
| Animalz | All twelve case studies report traditional organic metrics. The AEO page cites third-party market statistics instead of client results |
| Seer Interactive | Publishes only its own inbound figure, that 15% of inbound conversions came from someone who found Seer in an LLM. No client result |
| Minuttia | Runs three AI search service pages and publishes no AI result on any of them. Its case studies report organic visits and its service pages report client retention. It also returned 403 to our crawler, twice |
| Generate More | Its 22x headline is organic growth from AI-assisted content production, not AI search citation. A one-person advisory by its own description, whose two pricing pages contradict each other |
| Go Fish Digital | One blog post claiming ChatGPT Search displayed a "Notable Clients" section after a week. No number attached |
| OrganicHackers | No dedicated page. AI search is one bullet inside an ecommerce package, on an eleven-page site with no about page, no team page and no case studies |

## Why most GEO agency lists are unreliable

Every "best GEO agency" list we read was published by a GEO agency. Ten of the eleven rank themselves first. Here is what they get wrong.

**Go Fish Digital is not independent.** Agital [acquired it on 6 February 2024](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2024/02/06/2824407/0/en/Agital-Acquires-Go-Fish-Digital-Harnessing-Visionary-AI-Technologies-Award-Winning-SEO-Expertise.html), and Go Fish then absorbed EK Creative and Exclusive Concepts under its own brand. Four lists recommend it and none mentions the sale.

**RevenueZen is a subsidiary.** Onfolio Holdings bought substantially all of its assets for $1,105,000 on 4 January 2024, recorded in an [SEC 10-K filing](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1825452/000165495424004048/onfolio_10k.htm). Two lists present it as independent.

**Animalz has changed chief executive twice.** Founder Walter Chen is now Chairman and Ty Magnin is [listed as CEO](https://www.animalz.co/about). Two lists still describe a founder-led agency.

**Animalz is not in Auckland.** One list places it in New Zealand. [Animalz started in New York in 2015](https://www.animalz.co/about) and is remote, with leadership across the United States, Canada, Spain and the United Kingdom.

**Generate More gets its own price wrong.** [Its list](https://generatemore.ai/blog/top-generative-engine-optimization-agencies) quotes €2,500 to €10,000 a month for itself. Its [two](https://generatemore.ai/ai-visibility-aeo-pricing) [pricing pages](https://generatemore.ai/pricing) stop at €4,999 and $5,700, and disagree with each other on currency.

**Two founding years are simply wrong.** Lists give Go Fish Digital as 2009. The [acquisition release](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2024/02/06/2824407/0/en/Agital-Acquires-Go-Fish-Digital-Harnessing-Visionary-AI-Technologies-Award-Winning-SEO-Expertise.html) says 2005. Lists give Graphite as 2017 and 2019. [Its own press release](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/graphite-unveils-new-ai-driven-topical-seo-platform-302132427.html) says 2020.

**Directory prices do not match company prices.** Lister.ai publishes $1,699 and $1,500 a month for Embarque on the same page. Embarque's own site says $1,500.

